[
 {:seon.task/name "a-context-is-ordered-by-a-sort-key"
  :seon.task/plan "Approved by the owner, 2026-11-08: \"put it in everywhere we need them and remove all the fragile hacks\"; and \"why can't we use the sort by attributes location in the data to imply what you are sorting?\" Agreed by Context, Web and Optimization. No schema-layer or Datahike change, and no erase: the key is optional (owner: \"We are not requiring a sort key. It's optional and if it's there we can auto sort the results for the pull. This isn't a datahike thing it's a seon thing.\"), so no stored item becomes invalid.

What's wrong: the order of a context's items rests on Datahike issuing ids and transactions in order on one branch. Nothing states it, so a merge, a copy or content-hashed ids break it silently, and a window (`:limit` with `:reverse`) takes the newest n by eid. Neither Datahike nor Datomic orders a many-ref result (Web's reading of our fork's pull_api and Datomic's grammar).

The change:
1. One new attribute, `:seon.ctx/sort-key`, indexed (AVET), written by the context's process on every item whose order matters: a model call, an eval of any origin, a delivered message's copy on the branch. One key sequence per context process across all three kinds, so the chat's order is one sequence with no merging. From `seon.program/stable-places` (the mechanism `:form/order` uses): an insert moves no other, a re-run keeps its key, append takes the next gap. A message gets its key when the next turn takes it, so the model never meets it out of order. The key is optional in every schema: Seon sorts a pull's results by it when they carry it, and a list whose members carry none is a set, held in eid order only so its bytes are stable, with no meaning. The writers give it to every item whose order matters.
2. No new owner link: `:seon.eval/agent`, `:seon.model-call/agent` and a message's link stay. Where a list sits in the pulled tree (under its agent, under its turn) scopes its sort.
3. A list whose members carry the key is in that order, and `seon.ctx` sorts by it when it holds the list, reading it per member. Nothing is added to the pull syntax.
4. Windows: `seon.ctx` seeks `:seon.ctx/sort-key` newest first (rseek over AVET), keeping the items whose existing ref names the owner (`:seon.eval/agent`, `:seon.model-call/agent`, `:seon.message/to` containing it) until n are kept. Tuples aren't used: our schema layer cut them on purpose (`seon.schema.datahike`), and `:seon.message/to` is cardinality many, which a tuple can't hold. Optimization measured 2.8 k ticks, flat from 70 to 1,000 items, on an agent's own branch; on a branch holding several contexts the seek skips the others' items, a cost to measure there, and a tuple comes back as its own change only with numbers.
5. One key, three uses: sort on read, window by seek, and Back and a past request cut at a key.

The sweep, every order by eid or transaction of context items on main 066c67400f, each to read the key instead:
- Context: `seon.ctx/first-delivered` and `turn-members` (messages by delivering tx, evals by `(sort-by first ...)`), `seon.ctx.wire/redo-tx` (`(sort (keys (:evals c)))`), `seon.repl/again` and the eval before it (`(sort-by key (:evals @ctx/ctx))`).
- Web: `seon.render.ui`'s `(sort (keys (:evals c)))` (about line 1950), and `seon.root`'s newest agent of an id by `(sort-by :db/id)` (about line 296).
- Optimization: ctx's many-join maps held greatest-first by eid (docs/ctx.md), the `:limit :reverse` windows over root's agents and evals in seon.facts.edn's root fact, and the callers window (`[:form/_refs ... :limit :seon.config.ctx/callers :reverse true]`), whose newest-by-eid is arbitrary for content-id forms.
Fine as they are: calls sorted by `:seon.model-call/n` (seon.render.ui), sorted config keys and the like, which order no data.

Who does what: Optimization the schema entry first (a small merge the writers build on), the window seek and its cost proof; Context the writers (keys on calls, evals and delivered messages), the wire and Back reading by key, the bytes test and its part of the sweep; Web its part of the sweep and the two-agents proof.

Proofs, each red before the change: a reply's evals in key order with their eids scrambled; a message sent mid-turn placed after that turn's evals; two agents' items on one branch, each in its own order; a window of 10 over 1,000 items by key, its cost flat (seon.cost/proof); the request bytes unchanged on run 13's store copy once its items carry keys."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-shows-how-main-diverged"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:will-merge` (`seon.merge/divergence` against the agent's merge base) shows what the branch carries; nothing shows what main changed since that base, so a coming conflict is met only at merge. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §3.6, prototyped): one producer of main's changes since the base intersected with the branch's, shown every turn as {:main-ahead n :conflict [..] :you-call [..] :other n} (about 30 tokens): changed by both, changed on main and called by the branch's changes (:form/refs), the rest counted. Its cost follows the changes since the base, not the program (0.4-0.6 ms with a changed-keys index; a full scan 31 ms per db), so it needs the changed-since primitive of the index diff (`seon.merge/delta-changes`, docs/merging.md)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-turn-names-mains-coming-conflict}}
